{
  "id": "Compound::DB00777 - Gene::1128",
  "verbalization": "Propiomazine is likely to interact with Chrm1 through a shared pathway involving G-protein coupled receptors. The evidence suggests that Propiomazine binds to Chrm5, which participates in G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity, muscarinic acetylcholine receptor activity, or neurotransmitter receptor activity. These activities are all part of the broader G-protein coupled receptor (GPCR) family, which is known to mediate responses to acetylcholine and other neurotransmitters. The fact that Chrm1 is involved in these activities implies a potential role in signaling pathways downstream of GPCRs. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible mechanism by which Propiomazine may influence Chrm1 through its interaction with Chrm5, suggesting a role in modulating GPCR-mediated signaling.",
